module dev.slatelang.slate

import sh.sysl.gc
import sh.sysl.libuv

// `close` is a `Handle` member, and a trait has to be in scope to reach one. Every handle type in
// the binding needs it, so it is imported here rather than at each use.
import sh.sysl.libuv.Handle
import sh.sysl.parsing.*
import sysl.buf.{Buf, buf}

// The event loop, and what a slate program does with it.
//
// **A callback needs nothing from the machine, and that is worth knowing.** It runs *from* the loop,
// between statements of nothing, rather than from inside a half-evaluated expression -- so a
// tree-walker could have done all of this. `await` is the one that could not, because it suspends
// mid-expression, and it is built on top of what is here.
//
// ## The one thing embedding a loop costs a collected language
//
// **A pending callback is a root, and nothing else knows it.** `Timer.start` takes a sysl closure and
// the binding keeps it alive by reference counting -- but the *slate* closure that sysl closure means
// to call is a `Value` pointing into the collected heap, and the mark phase cannot see through an ARC
// box to find it. A timer set and then forgotten by the program is exactly the case: the only
// reference left is the one libuv holds, the collector frees the closure, and the timer eventually
// calls into swept memory.
//
// So the slate closure is held **here**, in a table this file roots, and the sysl closure captures a
// `usize` into it rather than the value. The same table holds the `&Timer`, because a counted box may
// not live in GC memory at all (see `obj.sysl`) and a slate program only ever sees the integer id.

// The libuv loop this line of execution's VM is on.
//
// **`machine()`'s shape, and it is here for `machine()`'s reason**: every site that starts a handle
// or turns the loop reads it, so routing them through one accessor is what let `default_loop()` stop
// being written in the runtime at all. libuv's default loop belongs to the process and may be turned
// by one thread only, so a VM on a thread of its own is on a loop of its own and every one of those
// sites has to ask which.
event_loop() -> &libuv.Loop = current().uv_loop

// One thing the loop is going to call back.
struct Pending
    // What to call. `Null` once the slot is spent, which is what makes it collectable again.
    callback: Value

    // What to call instead when the thing in this slot FAILS, and `Null` where nobody asked. Only a
    // socket has one: a timer cannot fail and a promise carries its own refusal. See `onError` in
    // `net.sysl` for why it is a registration of its own rather than a second argument to `onData`.
    on_error: Value

    // The promise to settle instead of calling anything, which is what `sleep` is. Null for a timer
    // a program set with `setTimeout`.
    promise: *PromiseObj

    // The handle, kept alive here for as long as the program may still cancel it.
    timer: Option[&libuv.Timer]

    // A socket, kept here for the same reason and closed the same way. **A socket differs from a
    // timer in what ends it**: a timer is spent when it fires and a socket lives until the program
    // says otherwise, so its slot is never surrendered by anything but `close`.
    socket: Option[&libuv.Tcp]

    // Whether this slot is in use. A spent slot is reused rather than grown past.
    live: bool

    // A repeating timer keeps firing; a one-shot frees its slot as it goes.
    repeating: bool

    // **Which occupant of this slot this is, and it is what makes an id safe to hand a program.**
    // A spent slot is reused, so an id on its own names a *position* rather than a thing: hold one
    // across a `close` and it comes to mean whatever took the position next. That is a use-after-free
    // wearing an integer's clothes, and it is silent -- `clearTimeout` on a cancelled timer's id
    // cancelled a *different* timer, and a closed socket's value compared equal to a live one.
    //
    // Every claim takes the next number, so a stale id carries a generation the slot no longer has
    // and every lookup misses. It is never reused and never reset.
    gen: usize

    // **WHICH HANDLE THIS ONE CAME OUT OF, as a generation, and its own where it came out of
    // nothing.** A connection a server accepts is the case: a listener is asked for connections once
    // and answers for as long as it is open, so a slot for one is claimed whenever a client happens to
    // dial -- which under `slate test` is inside whichever test was running. The connection belongs to
    // whoever opened the listener all the same.
    //
    // Read against a scope's own generation, it says whether a handle is that scope's: one whose owner
    // is older than the scope is something the scope came across rather than opened, so its drain does
    // not wait for it and `leaked_since` neither names nor closes it. Taking a connection for a
    // `@setupAll`'s server as a test's own is what made that test wait five seconds for a handle it
    // never asked for, report it as its leak, and close it under every test still to come.
    //
    // `inherit_scope` sets it, and passes an owner's owner along so that a chain of handles is
    // attributed to the one thing at the bottom of it.
    owner_gen: usize

// Everything the loop may still call is `current().pending`, how many callbacks are still owed is
// `current().outstanding`, and the number the next claim will stamp its slot with is
// `current().generation`. See `Pending.gen`, and `vm_state.sysl` for why the three are fields of the
// VM rather than module storage.
//
// **A collector root**: that table is the only thing standing between a pending callback and the
// sweep.

// Mark what the loop can still reach.
mark_pending(h: *gc.Heap)
    for i in 0..<current().pending.len()
        if current().pending.at(i).live
            mark_value(h, current().pending.at(i).callback)
            mark_value(h, current().pending.at(i).on_error)
            gc.mark(h, ptr_cast(current().pending.at(i).promise))

// Give the table back between runs.
reset_events()
    // **EVERY HANDLE IS CLOSED AND NOT MERELY DROPPED**, which is the rule `surrender` states and it
    // holds here for the same reason: a handle keeps a reference to itself while it is open, so
    // letting go of the last name for it stops nothing at all. Clearing the table was enough while
    // every program ended with its slots given back -- but a program stopped by a **fault** ends with
    // its sockets and its timers still live, and a watchdog firing is exactly that. The loop they
    // were made on is the *process's*, so a listener dropped open here is one the next program's
    // drain waits on, and nothing is ever going to connect to it: the wait has no end and nothing to
    // print. That is a hang in whichever program happened to run next, which is not the one with the
    // defect in it.
    for i in 0..<current().pending.len()
        surrender(i)

    current().pending.clear()
    forget_parsers()
    forget_tls()

    // **A signal watcher is not in this table and is put back anyway.** It is armed rather than
    // outstanding, so it lives in `signals.sysl`; leaving one running past its program would leave
    // the *process* -- which in the suite is the suite itself -- catching a `SIGTERM` on behalf of
    // something that has finished.
    forget_watches()

    // **A write to a peer that has gone is an ERROR and not the end of the process**, which is node's
    // rule and the one every `send` here is written against: a channel write answers a result, a
    // socket write rejects. The default action of `SIGPIPE` is to kill the process instead, before the
    // write can answer anything -- so a supervisor that wrote to a worker which had just exited, or a
    // server that wrote to a client which had just hung up, died with status 141 and nothing printed.
    // It is a race and not a mistake in the program: `cluster.sl`'s supervisor answering a worker's
    // hello after that worker had already been told to shut down lost it about once in sixty runs.
    // Asked here because every program starts here, and again after `forget_watches`, which may have
    // just closed a `SIGPIPE` watcher and put the default back.
    libuv.ignore_sigpipe()

    // **A derivation on the thread pool is rooted in `argon2.sysl` rather than here**, its job
    // being a box a pool thread holds a raw pointer into. It is put back for the reason everything
    // above it is: the next program starts with nothing of this one's still reachable.
    forget_argon2_jobs()

    // **An LMDB environment holds an open file and a lock on it**, so one left behind by a finished
    // program is a store the next one cannot open exclusively and a transaction still holding pages
    // the writer cannot reuse. The order inside is cursor, transaction, environment, which is what
    // LMDB requires and what `lmdb.sysl` says at length.
#if feature_lmdb
    forget_lmdb()
#endif

    // **A window left open is on the SCREEN**, which makes it the loudest thing in this list: the
    // suite is one process, so a window a faulted program never closed would still be up while the
    // next test ran, with the pump timer still turning its platform loop over.
#if feature_webview
    forget_windows()
#endif

    // **A database is not on the loop at all and is put back here anyway.** SQLite is synchronous, so
    // nothing of `slate:sqlite` is ever outstanding -- what a connection holds is a file lock and a
    // page cache, and leaving one open past its program would leave the *process*, which in the suite
    // is the suite itself, holding a lock on a database nobody is using.
    forget_sqlite()

    // **An actor is a THREAD, which is the same case one level up.** A thread is joined or it is
    // leaked -- `libuv.thread` keeps its body alive only while it runs and `join` is what drops it --
    // so an actor left behind is a thread of the next program's, on a loop and a heap that program
    // knows nothing about. Each is asked to stop, and each is waited for.
    forget_actors()

    // **A child is a PROCESS, so one left behind outlives the program that started it.** Its handles
    // are on the loop the next program runs on and nobody is watching it -- which in the suite means
    // the suite itself is the supervisor of a worker it never started.
    forget_children()
    current().outstanding = 0

    // **Back to zero, so that what a program is shown does not depend on what ran before it.** The
    // generation only has to be unique within one run -- nothing survives a `reset` to collide with
    // -- and letting it carry over would make a socket's printed name a function of the suite's
    // order.
    current().generation = 0
    current().scope_gen = 0
    current().loop_fault = None

// Take a slot, reusing a spent one where there is one.
//
// Not `private`: `private` in sysl is private to the *file*, and `fs.sysl` claims a slot for every
// request it has outstanding.
claim(p: Pending) -> usize
    val v = current()

    var slot = p

    v.generation += 1
    slot.gen = v.generation

    // A handle owns itself until something says it came out of another one, which only `inherit_scope`
    // ever does.
    slot.owner_gen = v.generation

    for i in 0..<v.pending.len()
        if !v.pending.at(i).live
            v.pending.set(i, slot)
            v.outstanding += 1
            return i

    v.pending.push(slot)
    v.outstanding += 1
    v.pending.len() - 1
end claim

// Say that the handle in `id` came out of the one in `owner`, so that what `owner` belongs to is what
// it belongs to. See `Pending.owner_gen`.
//
// **A connection a server accepted is the whole of this**, and the rule it states is that a listener's
// connections are the listener's: a test that opened one owns what it hands out, and a file that
// opened one in its `@setupAll` owns those for the whole file. `owner`'s own owner is what is passed
// along, so a listener that was itself inherited hands that on rather than claiming the connection
// for the scope it was accepted in.
inherit_scope(owner: usize, id: usize)
    val v = current()

    if owner >= v.pending.len() || id >= v.pending.len() then return

    var slot = v.pending.at(id)

    slot.owner_gen = v.pending.at(owner).owner_gen
    v.pending.set(id, slot)
end inherit_scope

// How many live slots this scope claimed on behalf of something older than it -- connections a
// listener from below handed over while this scope was current. A drain down to a floor may not wait
// for these, they being nothing this scope can close.
//
// **Only the ones claimed HERE**, which is what `gen > scope_gen` says. One claimed during an earlier
// test is already under this scope's floor, and counting it again would lift the floor past a handle
// this scope really did open.
private inherited_here() -> usize
    val v = current()

    // **A whole program is one scope with nothing below it**, so nothing in it can be owned from
    // outside and this is the answer without a walk. It matters: a server's own drain is the whole of
    // its running, and the table it would otherwise walk every turn is one entry per open connection.
    if v.scope_gen == 0 then return 0

    var n: usize = 0

    for i in 0..<v.pending.len()
        val slot = v.pending.at(i)

        if slot.live && slot.gen > v.scope_gen && slot.owner_gen <= v.scope_gen then n += 1

    n
end inherited_here

// Which occupant of a slot is in it now. `0` for a slot that has never been claimed, which no live
// id can carry -- `generation` counts from one.
//
// Not `private`: every id a program is given carries one of these, so `net.sysl` checks them too.
gen_at(id: usize) -> usize
    if id >= current().pending.len() then return 0

    current().pending.at(id).gen
end gen_at

// The slot a generation is in, if it is still in one.
//
// **A linear scan, and it is the right shape.** The table holds what the loop may still call, which
// is bounded by how much a program has in flight at once rather than by how much it has ever done --
// and the one caller is `clearTimeout`, which no program is in a hurry about.
slot_of_gen(gen: usize) -> Option[usize]
    for i in 0..<current().pending.len()
        val slot = current().pending.at(i)

        if slot.live && slot.gen == gen then return Some(i)

    None
end slot_of_gen

// Whether an id and the generation it was handed out with still name what they named.
holds(id: usize, gen: usize) -> bool
    if id >= current().pending.len() then return false

    val slot = current().pending.at(id)

    slot.live && slot.gen == gen
end holds

// Give a slot back. **The callback goes to `Null` here and not only the flag**, because the flag is
// what the mark phase reads and a live-looking slot would keep a dead closure alive forever.
//
// Not `private`: `fs.sysl` gives its slot back when an answer arrives.
surrender(id: usize)
    if id >= current().pending.len() then return

    var slot = current().pending.at(id)

    if !slot.live then return

    slot.live = false
    slot.callback = Null
    slot.promise = null

    // **The handle is CLOSED and not merely dropped.** A handle holds a reference to itself while it
    // is open, so letting go of the last name for it stops nothing -- deliberately, since dropping a
    // `setInterval` in JavaScript does not cancel it either. A handle never closed is also a handle
    // `run` will wait on forever, so this is what lets a program with a one-shot timer in it exit.
    slot.timer match
        Some(t) -> t.close(() -> ())
        None -> ()

    // A socket is closed for the same reason a timer is, and libuv insists on it for one more:
    // a handle that is never closed is one `run` waits on forever.
    slot.socket match
        Some(c) -> c.close(() -> ())
        None -> ()

    slot.timer = None
    slot.socket = None
    current().pending.set(id, slot)
    current().outstanding -= 1
end surrender

// Run the slate callback in a slot.
//
// **A fault inside a callback stops the program**, as an uncaught throw does in node. There is no
// statement to attach it to -- the loop is not running any -- so it is reported against the whole
// run.
private fire(id: usize)
    if id >= current().pending.len() then return

    val slot = current().pending.at(id)

    if !slot.live then return

    val cb = slot.callback
    val pr = slot.promise
    var none: Buf[Value] = buf()
    val mark = root_mark()

    hold(cb)

    // A one-shot is spent before its callback runs, so that a callback which sets another timer
    // cannot be given its own slot back underneath it. **The promise is read out first**, because
    // this is also what clears the slot's copy of it.
    if !slot.repeating then surrender(id)

    if pr != null
        // `sleep`: there is nothing to call, only a promise whose time has come.
        settle_ok(pr, Null)
    else
        apply_callback(cb, none, point(0), "setTimeout") match
            Ok(_) -> ()
            Err(s) -> current().loop_fault = Some(s)

    release(mark)
end fire

// The first fault a callback raised, if any, is `current().loop_fault`. Read once the loop stops.

// -- a request, which is a slot holding nothing but a promise ---------------------------------------

// **The other thing a slot can be, and the reason this table is not called the timer table.** A file
// system call and a socket's connect and send are each one attempt with one outcome, so each takes a
// slot that roots a promise and gives the slot back as the answer arrives. Nothing here knows what
// kind of request it is; `fs.sysl` and `net.sysl` are the two that do.
//
// **The slot is also what keeps the loop running.** `outstanding` counts it, so a program does not
// exit with a read still in the thread pool.

// Start a request: a promise, and the slot that roots it until libuv answers.
//
// The slot is what keeps the loop running too -- `outstanding` counts it, so a program does not exit
// with a read still in the thread pool.
begin_request() -> usize
    val mark = root_mark()
    val pr = new_promise()

    hold(Promise(pr))

    val id = claim(Pending(Null, Null, pr, None, None, true, false, 0, 0))

    release(mark)
    id
end begin_request

// The promise a slot is holding, as the value a program gets back.
awaited_request(id: usize) -> Value = Promise(current().pending.at(id).promise)

// Hand an answer back and give the slot up.
//
// **What settles is a result, not the value** -- `{ ok: true, value: v }` -- so a promise from a
// file or a socket never rejects and the caller is handed something it has to look at. The three
// functions below are the whole of that retrofit: every success in `fs.sysl` and `net.sysl` already
// came through here and every failure through the two under it.
answer_request(id: usize, v: Value)
    val mark = root_mark()

    hold(v)

    val wrapped = ok_value(v)

    release(mark)
    answer_plain_request(id, wrapped)
end answer_request

// The same, for the one call whose answer is not a result.
//
// **`exists` answers rather than failing and always could**, so wrapping it would be asking a caller
// to unpack a question that has no failure case.
//
// **The promise is read out before the slot is surrendered**, because surrendering is also what
// clears the slot's copy of it -- the same ordering `fire` needs and for the same reason.
answer_plain_request(id: usize, v: Value)
    if id >= current().pending.len() then return

    val slot = current().pending.at(id)

    if !slot.live then return

    val pr = slot.promise

    surrender(id)

    if pr != null then settle_ok(pr, v)
end answer_plain_request

// Fail a request, with the sentence libuv gave.
//
// **The promise SETTLES rather than rejecting**, carrying `{ ok: false, error: ... }`. The span is
// still taken because it says where the call was written, which is what a diagnostic would want if
// this ever went back to raising.
refuse_request(id: usize, at: Span, what: string, e: libuv.Error)
    if id >= current().pending.len() then return

    val slot = current().pending.at(id)

    if !slot.live then return

    val pr = slot.promise

    surrender(id)

    if pr != null then settle_ok(pr, err_value(s"${what}: ${e}"))
end refuse_request

// Fail a request with a sentence of slate's own, where there is no libuv error to quote.
deny_request(id: usize, at: Span, message: string)
    if id >= current().pending.len() then return

    val slot = current().pending.at(id)

    if !slot.live then return

    val pr = slot.promise

    surrender(id)

    if pr != null then settle_ok(pr, err_value(message))
end deny_request

// Reject a request's promise outright, where the promise was never going to carry a result.
//
// **`slate:crypto`'s Argon2 is the one caller and the reason is its own decision**: `argon2` answers the
// record itself rather than `{ ok, value }`, because a value the program built cannot fail to be
// read back -- the failures it does have are faults raised before anything is queued. So the one
// way the derivation can end badly is the pool refusing the job, and that has to reject rather than
// settle a shape the caller was never told to unpack.
//
// **The promise is read out before the slot is surrendered**, which is `answer_plain_request`'s
// ordering and is load-bearing for its reason.
//
// Not `private`: `argon2.sysl`'s completion callback is the caller.
fail_request(id: usize, message: string, at: Span)
    if id >= current().pending.len() then return

    val slot = current().pending.at(id)

    if !slot.live then return

    val pr = slot.promise

    surrender(id)

    if pr != null then settle_err(pr, message, at, current().current_source)
end fail_request

// Fail a request with a fault that crossed from ANOTHER thread's source table.
//
// **`fail_request`'s `current().current_source` is the wrong file entirely here** -- it names
// whatever this thread happens to be running, not the file the fault was actually raised in.
// `file` is that file's own NAME, resolved to an index THIS thread's table actually has by
// `local_source_of`, so the span that travelled beside it renders against the right text instead
// of a coincidence.
fail_request_elsewhere(id: usize, message: string, file: string, at: Span)
    if id >= current().pending.len() then return

    val slot = current().pending.at(id)

    if !slot.live then return

    val pr = slot.promise

    surrender(id)

    if pr != null then settle_err(pr, message, at, local_source_of(file))
end fail_request_elsewhere

// A request libuv would not even start is one whose callback will never run, so the promise it was
// given has to fail here instead of waiting forever.
request_started(id: usize, at: Span, what: string, r: Result[unit, libuv.Error]) -> Step
    // **THE PROMISE IS READ BEFORE THE REQUEST CAN BE REFUSED, AND THE ORDER IS THE WHOLE OF IT.**
    // Refusing surrenders the slot, and surrendering clears the slot's copy of the promise -- so
    // `awaited_request` afterwards answers `Promise(null)`, which the machine dereferences and the
    // process dies on a bare SIGSEGV with nothing said. It is the failure path of every call that
    // can be refused before it starts, which is why it went unnoticed: libuv answers nearly
    // everything asynchronously and this arm is only taken when it refuses on the spot.
    val promised = awaited_request(id)

    r match
        Ok(_) -> Ok(promised)
        Err(e) ->
            refuse_request(id, at, what, e)
            Ok(promised)
end request_started

// The same check, for a step taken from inside a callback rather than from the builtin.
//
// **A chain of requests has one promise and several chances to fail to start.** `request_started`
// answers the value the builtin hands back; this one has nobody to answer, and exists only so that a
// step libuv refused fails the promise instead of leaving it pending for the rest of the program.
request_continued(id: usize, at: Span, what: string, r: Result[unit, libuv.Error])
    r match
        Ok(_) -> ()
        Err(e) -> refuse_request(id, at, what, e)

// -- what a program calls --------------------------------------------------------------------------

// `setTimeout(fn, ms)` and `setInterval(fn, ms)`, which differ only in the repeat.
set_timer(cb: Value, ms: long, repeating: bool, at: Span) -> Step
    cb match
        Fn(_) -> ()
        Native(_) -> ()
        HostFn(_) -> ()
        other -> return fault(at, s"a timer needs a function to call, and this is ${other.type_name()}")

    val delay = if ms < 0 then 0u64 else u64(ms)
    val every = if repeating then delay else 0u64

    libuv.timer(event_loop()) match
        Err(e) -> fault(at, s"this timer could not be made: ${e}")
        Ok(t) ->
            val id = claim(Pending(cb, Null, null, Some(t), None, true, repeating, 0, 0))

            // **The closure captures the id and not the value**, which is the whole arrangement: the
            // collector cannot see through this box, so what it holds must be something the collector
            // does not have to see.
            t.start(delay, every, () -> fire(id)) match
                Err(e) ->
                    surrender(id)
                    fault(at, s"this timer could not be started: ${e}")
                Ok(_) -> Ok(Int(long(gen_at(id))))
end set_timer

// `sleep(ms)` -- a promise that settles when the time is up.
//
// **This is the one builtin that makes `await` worth having on its own.** Everything else a program
// can build for itself out of an `async` function; waiting for the clock needs a handle, and a handle
// needs the table above.
sleep_promise(ms: long, at: Span) -> Step
    val delay = if ms < 0 then 0u64 else u64(ms)

    libuv.timer(event_loop()) match
        Err(e) -> fault(at, s"this timer could not be made: ${e}")
        Ok(t) ->
            val pr = new_promise()
            val mark = root_mark()

            hold(Promise(pr))

            val id = claim(Pending(Null, Null, pr, Some(t), None, true, false, 0, 0))

            release(mark)

            t.start(delay, 0u64, () -> fire(id)) match
                Err(e) ->
                    surrender(id)
                    fault(at, s"this timer could not be started: ${e}")
                Ok(_) -> Ok(Promise(pr))
end sleep_promise

// `clearTimeout(id)` and `clearInterval(id)`.
//
// **The number a program holds is the GENERATION and not the slot**, which is the whole of what
// makes cancelling safe. A slot is reused, so cancelling a timer by its position cancelled whatever
// had since taken the position -- silently, and the program relying on that second timer simply
// never heard from it. A generation is never reused, so a stale id finds nothing.
//
// That it is a number at all is node's convention. Nothing but this pair of functions reads it, and
// nothing about it is promised beyond being able to name one timer once.
clear_timer(v: Value, at: Span) -> Step
    v match
        Int(n) ->
            if n >= 0
                slot_of_gen(usize(n)) match
                    Some(id) -> surrender(id)
                    None -> ()

            Ok(Null)
        other -> fault(at, s"a timer is cancelled by its id, and this is ${other.type_name()}")

// Everything claimed since `gen_floor` and still open, closed and named -- called AFTER the ordinary
// drain has already had its turn, so this finds something only where that drain gave up on it.
//
// **Walked by GENERATION, not by count.** `outstanding` says how many slots are live and nothing
// about which ones: a slot spent and reused between two tests leaves the count exactly where it was
// while naming something new. `Pending.gen` only ever goes up, so a slot claimed after the floor is a
// slot this scope opened, whether or not its number was reused from one that closed cleanly. Closing
// it here is what keeps a leak from being inherited by whatever runs next -- the next test's own
// floor, or the file's drain after `@teardownAll` -- and naming it here is what turns a bare timeout
// into a report that says WHAT was left rather than only how long the wait was.
leaked_since(gen_floor: usize) -> string
    var named: Buf[string] = buf()

    for i in 0..<current().pending.len()
        val slot = current().pending.at(i)

        // **A handle owned from below is not this scope's to name or to close.** A connection a
        // `@setupAll`'s server accepted while this test ran was claimed after the floor and is the
        // file's, and closing it here would take it away from every test still to come. See
        // `Pending.owner_gen`.
        if slot.live && slot.gen > gen_floor && slot.owner_gen > gen_floor
            named.push(leak_description(slot))
            surrender(i)

    if named.is_empty() then return ""

    var joined = named.at(0)

    for i in 1..<named.len()
        joined = s"${joined}, ${named.at(i)}"

    val word = if named.len() == 1 then "handle" else "handles"

    s"left ${named.len()} ${word} open: ${joined}"
end leaked_since

// What a leaked slot was, for the sentence `leaked_since` reports.
private leak_description(slot: Pending) -> string
    slot.socket match
        Some(sock) -> sock.sockname() match
            Err(_) -> "a socket"
            Ok(addr) -> sock.peername() match
                Ok(_) -> s"a TCP connection on :${addr.port()}"
                Err(_) -> s"a TCP server on :${addr.port()}"
        None -> slot.timer match
            Some(_) -> "a timer"
            None -> "a handle"
end leak_description

// Run until nothing is outstanding and nothing is owed.
//
// **This is what makes a slate program with a timer in it not exit immediately.** node does the same:
// the process lives as long as something is scheduled.
//
// **Two queues, drained in a fixed order, and the order is the whole rule.** Everything a suspended
// call is owed is handed back *before* the loop is allowed another turn -- so a promise resolved
// inside a timer callback moves its awaiting function along before the next timer fires. Running
// libuv to completion in one go, as this used to, would have batched every callback ahead of every
// resumption and interleaved them in an order nothing could predict. JavaScript's microtask rule is
// exactly this, and it is why `RUN_ONCE` is asked for rather than the default.
// **AND THE PROGRAM IS THE FIRST ACTOR, so the drain is not over while it has spawned another.** The
// design's rule is that the program ends when its own loop has drained and every actor it spawned
// has stopped; what makes that terminate rather than hang is that a drained program has nothing left
// to say to an actor, so each is asked to stop -- orderly, which drains its mailbox and runs its own
// loop down -- and then waited for. An answer arriving in that wait can resume a coroutine that
// starts more work, which is why this is a loop and not two steps.
drain_events() -> Result[unit, Signal]
    loop
        drain_events_to(0, false)?

        if !finish_actors()? then return Ok(())
end drain_events

// Close a bounded drain's watchdog timer, where it armed one. Not `pending`-tracked and not
// counted in `outstanding`, so this is the only thing that ever gives it back.
private close_watchdog(watchdog: Option[&libuv.Timer])
    watchdog match
        Some(t) -> t.close(() -> ())
        None -> ()

// The same drain, stopping once nothing beyond `floor` is outstanding.
//
// **A FLOOR IS WHAT LETS A DRAIN BELONG TO PART OF A PROGRAM RATHER THAN TO ALL OF IT.** `slate
// test` runs a file's `@setupAll`, then each test, then its `@teardownAll`, and a store or a
// listener the `@setupAll` opened is meant to stay open for the whole file -- so a test's drain
// waiting for *nothing* outstanding waits for that listener to close and never returns. What a
// test's drain is owed is what the test itself left behind, which is everything above the count
// standing when it began.
//
// **`bounded` is SAID, not read off `floor`.** The obvious shortcut -- `floor == 0` means the whole
// program -- is wrong: a per-test drain's floor is `current().outstanding` at the moment the test
// began, which is 0 for the ordinary case of a file with no `@setupAll` and nothing outstanding yet.
// So a test-scoped drain and a whole-program one are not told apart by the NUMBER, only by which one
// the caller meant, and only the caller knows that.
drain_events_to(floor: usize, bounded: bool) -> Result[unit, Signal]
    // How many turns in a row have reported nothing active and settled nothing of ours. See the
    // guard below for what it is for.
    var idle = 0

    // **A per-scope drain is bounded; the whole program's is not.** `run` and `prepare` drain an
    // ENTIRE program, where a server that never closes its own listener is running exactly as it was
    // written to -- there is no time after which that becomes wrong. A per-test drain's ordinary
    // case is a handle that is ALWAYS going to close on its own -- a backoff timer a moment from
    // firing -- which this waits out exactly as it always did. The bound is what stops that same
    // wait for a handle that is NEVER going to close: `run_guarded` reads a timeout here and calls
    // `leaked_since` to say what was still open and close it, so a leak becomes a named failure at a
    // few seconds rather than an unbounded hang.
    val started = libuv.hrtime()
    val backstop_ms: u64 = 5000

    // **THE BACKSTOP CHECK BELOW IS NOT ENOUGH ON ITS OWN, because `RUN_ONCE` BLOCKS ON WHAT IT IS
    // ASKED TO.** `uv_run(UV_RUN_ONCE)` waits for the nearest thing that could wake it -- a timer
    // due, or an fd becoming readable -- and a listening socket that nobody ever connects to offers
    // neither. With no timer of the PROGRAM's own outstanding, that wait has no upper bound, so the
    // elapsed-time check a few lines down never gets a turn to run again: the process blocks inside
    // the poll syscall, at 0% CPU, for as long as the leaked handle stays open. A raw watchdog timer
    // -- outside `pending`, so it is invisible to `outstanding` and to a leak report -- gives that
    // wait a ceiling: libuv computes `RUN_ONCE`'s timeout from its OWN nearest timer, so one due at
    // `backstop_ms` is what makes the poll return on schedule even when nothing else ever will.
    //
    // **The due time is counted from the loop's CACHED clock, so the clock is read first.** The cache
    // was last updated before the test body ran; a body that took a while, or a machine too busy to
    // run it promptly, leaves it behind, and a watchdog counted from it fires before `started +
    // backstop_ms`. The check below then says "not yet" and the next turn blocks for ever, a one-shot
    // watchdog being spent. `update_time` removes that, and the REPEAT is what keeps a watchdog armed
    // for the millisecond the loop's whole-millisecond clock can still leave between the two: once
    // due, it wakes the loop every `rewake_ms` until the check below is true.
    val rewake_ms: u64 = 100
    var watchdog: Option[&libuv.Timer] = None

    if bounded
        event_loop().update_time()

        libuv.timer(event_loop()) match
            Ok(t) ->
                t.start(backstop_ms, rewake_ms, () -> ()) match
                    Ok(_) -> watchdog = Some(t)
                    Err(_) -> t.close(() -> ())
            Err(_) -> ()

    loop
        run_microtasks()

        // A resumption may have faulted its way out to a promise nobody holds, and a callback may
        // have faulted outright. Either ends the program.
        current().loop_fault match
            Some(s) ->
                close_watchdog(watchdog)
                return Err(s)
            None -> ()

        // **What this drain is owed is the floor plus whatever it took on for somebody else.** A
        // connection this scope's turns accepted for a listener a lower one opened is claimed here
        // and belongs there, so it lifts the floor for as long as it is live -- read every turn,
        // because a turn of the loop is exactly when another one can arrive. See `Pending.owner_gen`.
        val owed = floor + inherited_here()

        if current().outstanding <= owed then break

        if bounded && libuv.hrtime() - started > backstop_ms * 1000000
            close_watchdog(watchdog)
            return Err(failure(point(0), s"the event loop did not drain: ${current().outstanding - owed} handle(s) still open after ${backstop_ms}ms"))

        // **A turn reporting nothing active does NOT end the drain, and thinking it did cost a
        // working file system.** A timer is a handle and libuv counts it, so a loop with one
        // pending says it is alive and the count and the loop agree. **An `fs` call is a
        // REQUEST**: the turn that answers the last one outstanding processes its callback and
        // *then* reports nothing active, so breaking on that answer throws away everything the
        // callback settled -- the promise resolves, and the coroutine awaiting it is never handed
        // its value because `run_microtasks` at the top of this loop is what does the handing.
        //
        // `outstanding` is the authority on whether there is work, and the loop's answer is only a
        // check on it. Two turns running with nothing active and nothing of ours settled means the
        // count is wrong, and stopping is better than spinning on it.
        if idle >= 2 then break

        // **Read a turn beginning HERE and not after the last one ended.** `run_microtasks` above
        // resumes coroutines, and a resumed coroutine starts the next request -- so a count taken
        // at the bottom of the loop is a count of work this turn had nothing to do with.
        val before = current().outstanding

        val more = event_loop().run(libuv.RUN_ONCE) match
            Err(e) ->
                close_watchdog(watchdog)
                return Err(failure(point(0), s"the event loop stopped: ${e}"))
            Ok(alive) -> alive

        idle = if !more && current().outstanding == before then idle + 1 else 0
    end loop

    // The watchdog's job ends with the loop above -- the closing turn below is `RUN_NOWAIT` for a
    // bounded drain, which never blocks at all, so nothing past this point can hang.
    close_watchdog(watchdog)

    // A handle that was closed needs one more turn for its close callback, and there is nothing left
    // to wait for -- so this returns as soon as those have run.
    //
    // **`RUN_NOWAIT` FOR A BOUNDED DRAIN, BECAUSE THE DEFAULT MODE RUNS UNTIL THE LOOP IS EMPTY.**
    // A per-scope drain stopped at a floor that may still have a handle open above it by
    // construction, and asking the default mode for one more turn there is asking it to wait for
    // that handle to close -- which is the very wait the floor exists to avoid. **This reads
    // `bounded` and not `floor`**, for the reason above: a per-test drain's floor can be zero too.
    val mode = if bounded then libuv.RUN_NOWAIT else libuv.RUN_DEFAULT

    val closed: Result[unit, Signal] = event_loop().run(mode) match
        Err(e) -> Err(failure(point(0), s"the event loop stopped: ${e}"))
        Ok(_) -> Ok(())

    closed?

    // That last turn may itself have settled something, and a promise settled with nobody handed
    // the answer is a coroutine abandoned mid-statement.
    run_microtasks()

    current().loop_fault match
        Some(s) -> Err(s)
        None -> Ok(())
end drain_events_to

// Run the loop only until this one call has finished -- not until the loop has nothing left.
//
// **THIS IS WHAT LETS A `@setup`, ITS TEST AND ITS `@teardown` SHARE ONE EVENT-LOOP SCOPE.** Each of
// the three has to finish before the next begins, and an `async` one finishes when its promise
// settles; draining to idle in between would ALSO wait out everything the phase deliberately left
// standing -- a listener the setup opened, a timer the test armed for its teardown to clear. So this
// waits for the promise and for nothing else, and the drain that waits for the rest happens once,
// after the teardown has had its chance to give back what the setup took.
//
// A value that is not a promise has nothing to wait for; the microtasks are still run, a call that
// settled something on its way out owing the resumption.
settle_call(v: Value) -> Result[unit, Signal]
    v match
        Promise(pr) -> settle_promise(pr)
        _ ->
            run_microtasks()

            current().loop_fault match
                Some(s) -> Err(s)
                None -> Ok(())

// Turn the loop until `pr` settles, or until nothing is left that could ever settle it.
//
// **Nothing outstanding is the answer "never", and it is the one this can give.** A promise waiting
// on a handle that is never going to fire cannot be told from one waiting on a handle that is about
// to, so a loop with work in it is turned; a loop with none is a promise the caller reports as a
// call that never finished.
private settle_promise(pr: *PromiseObj) -> Result[unit, Signal]
    var idle = 0

    loop
        run_microtasks()

        current().loop_fault match
            Some(s) -> return Err(s)
            None -> ()

        if pr.settled then break
        if current().outstanding == 0 then break
        if idle >= 2 then break

        val before = current().outstanding

        val more = event_loop().run(libuv.RUN_ONCE) match
            Err(e) -> return Err(failure(point(0), s"the event loop stopped: ${e}"))
            Ok(alive) -> alive

        idle = if !more && current().outstanding == before then idle + 1 else 0
    end loop

    Ok(())
end settle_promise
